## Reasons / Findings

### 1\. Out-of-Bounds `Array.insert(_:at:)` with an Invalid Index

Frame 1 (`Array.insert(_:at:) + 346`) combined with `_assertionFailure` is the Swift runtime&#39;s definitive signature for inserting into an array at an index greater than `array.count`. At line 682 of `updatePrice()`, the code computes or assumes an insertion index (likely from the API response or a local counter) that no longer matches the actual array size — possibly because the array was modified or reloaded between the time the network request was fired and when its callback executed.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Crashes when index > array.count or array was mutated during the network call
func updatePrice() {
    // ...
    priceFilters.insert(newEntry, at: serverReturnedIndex)
}

// ✅ Clamp the index before inserting, or append when out of range
func updatePrice() {
    let safeIndex = min(serverReturnedIndex, priceFilters.count)
    priceFilters.insert(newEntry, at: safeIndex)
}

// ✅ Alternative — use append if order doesn't matter, then sort
func updatePrice() {
    priceFilters.append(newEntry)
    priceFilters.sort { $0.order < $1.order }
}
```

### 2\. Array Mutated by a Concurrent UI Reload Between Request Dispatch and Callback

The `priceAbsurdCheck` network call is dispatched when the user submits a price. If a pull-to-refresh, a tab switch, or a Realm notification triggers a full data reload before the network response arrives, the array that `updatePrice()` operates on is replaced or emptied. The insertion index computed before the reload is now invalid for the new array.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Index captured at request time is stale by callback time
func checkAbsurdPrice(price: String, unit: String, completion: @escaping () -> Void) {
    let targetIndex = currentInsertionIndex  // captured now
    MyProductsManager.shared.priceAbsurdCheck(...) { result in
        self.priceFilters.insert(result, at: targetIndex)  // array may have changed
    }
}

// ✅ Re-derive the index inside the callback, after confirming array state
func checkAbsurdPrice(price: String, unit: String, completion: @escaping () -> Void) {
    MyProductsManager.shared.priceAbsurdCheck(...) { [weak self] result in
        guard let self else { return }
        // Re-compute index from current array state, not from a stale capture
        let safeIndex = min(derivedIndex(for: result), priceFilters.count)
        priceFilters.insert(result, at: safeIndex)
        completion()
    }
}
```

### 3\. Missing `weak self` / Stale Cell Reference in Closure

`ProductListCellFilterSpecific` is a cell — it can be reused or deallocated by `UITableView`/`UICollectionView` while the network request is in flight. If the closure captures `self` strongly, it keeps a deallocated or recycled cell alive. The array it then mutates (`self.priceFilters`) belongs to a cell that is no longer displaying the original product, making the index semantically wrong even if it&#39;s numerically in-bounds.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Strong capture keeps a recycled cell alive; mutates the wrong data
MyProductsManager.shared.priceAbsurdCheck(...) {
    self.updatePrice()  // 'self' may be a recycled cell showing a different product
}

// ✅ Weak capture — bail out if cell was recycled or deallocated
MyProductsManager.shared.priceAbsurdCheck(...) { [weak self] result in
    guard let self, isRepresentingOriginalProduct() else { return }
    updatePrice()
}

// Track which product this cell was showing when the request was fired
private func isRepresentingOriginalProduct() -> Bool {
    currentProductId == productIdAtRequestTime
}
```

### 4\. Network Response Delivered Without Explicit Main Queue Dispatch

`NetworkManager.jsonReqJSONData` at line 710 calls its success closure. If this closure is not explicitly dispatched to `DispatchQueue.main`, Alamofire may deliver it on a background queue. `updatePrice()` then mutates a UI-bound array from a non-main thread — which is both a threading violation and a data race that can corrupt the array&#39;s internal state, causing the bounds assertion to fire non-deterministically.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ No explicit queue — Alamofire may deliver on a background thread
func jsonReqJSONData(..., success: @escaping (Data) -> Void, ...) {
    AF.request(...).responseData { response in
        success(response.data!)
    }
}

// ✅ Always deliver UI-touching callbacks on the main queue
func jsonReqJSONData(..., success: @escaping (Data) -> Void, ...) {
    AF.request(...).responseData(queue: .main) { response in
        guard let data = response.value else { return }
        success(data)
    }
}

// ✅ Or enforce at the call site with @MainActor
@MainActor
func updatePrice() {
    // Swift compiler enforces main thread — no runtime crash possible
    priceFilters.insert(newEntry, at: safeIndex)
}
```

## References

*   [Array.insert(\_:at:) — Swift Standard Library (Apple Docs)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swift/array/insert(_:at:)) — Documents the precondition: index must be `<= endIndex`; violating it triggers a fatal error.
*   [Closures — Capture Lists in Swift (Apple Docs)](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/closures/#Capture-Lists) — Explains `[weak self]` capture semantics to prevent stale object mutations from async callbacks.
*   [Alamofire — Response Queue Dispatch (GitHub Docs)](https://github.com/Alamofire/Alamofire/blob/master/Documentation/AdvancedUsage.md#response-queue) — Shows how to specify `queue: .main` on response handlers to ensure UI mutations happen on the correct thread.
*   [MainActor — Swift Concurrency (Apple Docs)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swift/mainactor) — Compile-time enforcement that a function runs on the main thread, eliminating the entire class of threading + bounds crash.
*   [WWDC 2021 — ARC in Swift: Basics and Beyond (Session 10216)](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2021/10216/) — Covers closure capture semantics and how strong captures of cells lead to unexpected retain cycles and stale mutations.

## Overview

The app crashes when a user taps a push notification that is meant to open the Lead Manager screen. The notification tap is intercepted by MoEngage before reaching the app, and by the time the app tries to display the Lead Manager screen, a UI layer it depends on has already been torn down or replaced — causing a fatal graphics crash. This affects any seller who taps a lead-related push notification while the app is in the background or transitioning between screens.

