ate.swift</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">1372</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">moveToRespectiveController()</code></td></tr><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">AppDelegate.swift</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">1285</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">userNotificationCenter(_:didReceive:withCompletionHandler:)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>

## Reasons / Findings

### 1\. View Added to a Deallocated or Mid-Transition Layer Inside `openLeadManagerScreen`

`CALayerInvalid` is thrown by QuartzCore when `addSublayer` is called on a `CALayer` whose parent layer tree has already been invalidated — typically because the owning `UIView` or `UIViewController` was deallocated or is mid-teardown. At line 633 of `openLeadManagerScreen`, the code likely retrieves the current root/tab view controller and immediately pushes or presents a view on it. If a previous screen transition (triggered by `moveToRespectiveController` at line 1372) is still animating, the layer being targeted is in an invalid state.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Pushes a new screen while a prior transition is still in flight
func openLeadManagerScreen(for leadId: String, ...) {
    let vc = LeadManagerViewController(leadId: leadId)
    navigationController?.pushViewController(vc, animated: true)  // layer may be mid-transition
}

// ✅ Guard against in-flight transitions before pushing
func openLeadManagerScreen(for leadId: String, ...) {
    guard let nav = navigationController, !nav.transitionCoordinator.map({ $0.isAnimated }) ?? false else {
        // Defer until transition completes
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 0.3) { [weak self] in
            self?.openLeadManagerScreen(for: leadId, ...)
        }
        return
    }
    let vc = LeadManagerViewController(leadId: leadId)
    nav.pushViewController(vc, animated: true)
}
```

### 2\. MoEngage Method Swizzling Delivering the Notification Callback on a Non-Main Thread

`MoEngageObjCUtils.moe_swizzle_didReceiveNotificationResponseWithHandler` sits directly in the call chain at frame 32. MoEngage swizzles `userNotificationCenter(_:didReceive:withCompletionHandler:)` and, depending on the SDK version, may invoke the original implementation on a background thread. All subsequent navigation code in `AppDelegate` and `IMTabViewController+Redirection` then executes off the main thread — mutating `CALayer` from a non-main thread triggers `CALayerInvalid`.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Navigation triggered directly inside the delegate — may be on a background thread
func userNotificationCenter(
    _ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,
    didReceive response: UNNotificationResponse,
    withCompletionHandler completionHandler: @escaping () -> Void
) {
    moveToRespectiveController()  // unsafe if MoEngage calls this off main thread
    completionHandler()
}

// ✅ Explicitly marshal all navigation work to the main queue
func userNotificationCenter(
    _ center: UNUserNotificationCenter,
    didReceive response: UNNotificationResponse,
    withCompletionHandler completionHandler: @escaping () -> Void
) {
    DispatchQueue.main.async { [weak self] in
        self?.moveToRespectiveController()
    }
    completionHandler()
}
```

### 3\. `moveToRespectiveController` Initiates a Tab Switch and Push Simultaneously

The call chain shows `moveToRespectiveController` (AppDelegate:1372) → `pushReceived` → `redirection` → `openLeadManager` → `openLeadManagerScreen` — four navigation mutations chained synchronously. If `moveToRespectiveController` switches the selected tab while `openLeadManagerScreen` immediately pushes onto the new tab&#39;s navigation controller, the tab transition and the push animation both manipulate the layer tree at the same time, leaving a layer in an invalid intermediate state.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Tab switch + push happen in the same run loop tick
func moveToRespectiveController() {
    tabBarController.selectedIndex = targetTab
    pushReceived(data: pendingPushData)  // pushes before tab transition settles
}

// ✅ Wait for the tab transition to complete before pushing
func moveToRespectiveController() {
    tabBarController.selectedIndex = targetTab
    // Allow the tab animation to complete before driving further navigation
    DispatchQueue.main.async { [weak self] in
        self?.pushReceived(data: self?.pendingPushData)
    }
}
```

### 4\. `IMTabViewController` Accessed Before Its View Is Loaded

`IMTabViewController+Redirection` at line 633 calls into what is likely a `UINavigationController` or child view controller of the tab. If the tab was not previously selected (i.e., its view has never been loaded), accessing `viewController.view.layer` before `loadViewIfNeeded()` is called returns a layer attached to an uninitialized view hierarchy. Adding sublayers to it throws `CALayerInvalid`.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Accesses the VC's layer before its view is guaranteed to be loaded
func openLeadManagerScreen(for leadId: String, ...) {
    let targetVC = tabBarController.viewControllers?[targetIndex] as? UINavigationController
    targetVC?.view.layer.addSublayer(overlayLayer)  // view may not be loaded yet
}

// ✅ Force the view to load before accessing its layer
func openLeadManagerScreen(for leadId: String, ...) {
    guard let targetVC = tabBarController.viewControllers?[targetIndex] as? UINavigationController else { return }
    targetVC.loadViewIfNeeded()  // safe no-op if already loaded
    let vc = LeadManagerViewController(leadId: leadId)
    targetVC.pushViewController(vc, animated: true)
}
```

