*   [CALayer — Apple Developer Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/quartzcore/calayer) — Documents the threading rule: all CALayer mutations must occur on the main thread.
*   [UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate — Apple Developer Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/usernotifications/unusernotificationcenterdelegate) — Specifies that delegate callbacks are delivered on the main queue by default; third-party swizzling may violate this.
*   [UIViewController.loadViewIfNeeded() — Apple Developer Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/uiviewcontroller/1621446-loadviewifneeded) — Safe API to ensure a view controller&#39;s view is loaded before accessing its layer or subviews.
*   [UINavigationController.transitionCoordinator — Apple Developer Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/uiviewcontroller/1619294-transitioncoordinator) — Use this to detect in-flight transitions and defer navigation until they complete.
*   [WWDC 2018 — iOS Memory Deep Dive (Session 416)](https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2018/416/) — Covers view lifecycle and layer invalidation patterns that lead to `CALayerInvalid` crashes.
*   [MoEngage iOS SDK — Push Notification Handling](https://developers.moengage.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403895168788-Push-Notifications) — Documents how MoEngage swizzles notification delegate methods and recommended integration patterns to avoid threading issues.

## Overview

The app crashes intermittently while running in the foreground when Firebase Remote Config&#39;s realtime update listener receives a network response containing fragmented JSON data. The crash happens inside Firebase&#39;s own SDK code — not in app-authored code — when it tries to extract a substring from malformed chunk boundaries, resulting in an invalid (negative-length) string range. This is a known Firebase SDK defect fixed in version 11.10.0; updating the SDK will eliminate the crash entirely.

[View in Firebase Crashlytics →](https://console.firebase.google.com/u/0/project/artful-affinity-634/crashlytics/app/ios:com.indiamart.m/issues/c55952bd7c6ff966dad7fad3e3116962?time=7d&versions=13.6.4%20(4)&types=crash&sessionEventKey=372e769aa35545b18e018ce030f721f1_2194010899691768003)

## Relevant Code / Files Involved

<figure class="table op-uc-figure_align-center op-uc-figure"><table class="op-uc-table"><thead class="op-uc-table--head"><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><th class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell op-uc-table--cell_head">File</th><th class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell op-uc-table--cell_head">Line</th><th class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell op-uc-table--cell_head">Method</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">RCNConfigRealtime.m</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">565</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">-[RCNConfigRealtime URLSession:dataTask:didReceiveData:]</code></td></tr><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">FPRNSURLSessionDelegateInstrument.m</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">122</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">__InstrumentURLSessionDataTaskDidReceiveData_block_invoke</code></td></tr><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">RCNConfigDBManager.m</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">467</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">-[RCNConfigDBManager insertMainTableWithValues:fromSource:]</code></td></tr><tr class="op-uc-table--row"><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">AppDelegate.swift</code></td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell">29</td><td class="op-uc-p op-uc-table--cell"><code class="op-uc-code">main</code></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>

## Reasons / Findings

### 1\. Fragmented JSON Parsing in RCNConfigRealtime (Primary Cause)

Firebase Remote Config&#39;s realtime listener receives streamed HTTP chunks via `URLSession:dataTask:didReceiveData:`. The SDK scans each chunk for `{` and `}` delimiters to extract JSON objects, but assumes both delimiters always appear in order within every chunk. When the network splits data mid-JSON (e.g., `}` arrives in a chunk before `{`, or only one delimiter is present), the computed `NSRange` length becomes negative, and `substringWithRange:` crashes with &quot;CFString cannot be created from a negative number of bytes.&quot; This is tracked as [firebase-ios-sdk #14094](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk/issues/14094) and fixed in [PR #14518](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk/pull/14518).

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Current SDK version — no guard on delimiter ordering
// The app bundles a firebase-ios-sdk version < 11.10.0 that has this bug.

// ✅ Update firebase-ios-sdk to 11.10.0+ (latest stable: 12.10.0)

// Swift Package Manager — Package.swift
.package(
    url: "https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk.git",
    from: "12.0.0"
)

// CocoaPods — Podfile
// pod 'Firebase/RemoteConfig', '~> 12.0'
```

### 2\. Temporary Mitigation — Replace Realtime Listener with Polling

If an immediate SDK update is not possible (e.g., regression testing timelines), the crash can be avoided entirely by removing the realtime config update listener and falling back to periodic fetch-and-activate calls. This removes the `RCNConfigRealtime` code path from execution.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Realtime listener triggers the vulnerable code path
remoteConfig.addOnConfigUpdateListener { configUpdate, error in
    guard error == nil else { return }
    remoteConfig.activate { _, _ in }
}

// ✅ Polling-based fetch avoids RCNConfigRealtime entirely
func fetchRemoteConfig() {
    remoteConfig.fetch(withExpirationDuration: 3600) { status, error in
        guard status == .success, error == nil else { return }
        remoteConfig.activate { changed, error in
            guard error == nil else { return }
            // Apply updated config values
        }
    }
}
```

### 3\. Firebase Performance Instrumentation Swizzling on the Crash Path

The stack trace shows `FPRNSURLSessionDelegateInstrument` (Firebase Performance SDK) wrapping the `didReceiveData:` delegate callback before it reaches `RCNConfigRealtime`. While this is not the root cause, the swizzled interception adds an extra layer on the main thread. If Firebase Performance monitoring is not critical for Remote Config&#39;s internal network calls, disabling automatic URL session instrumentation can reduce overhead on the crash path.

**Recommended Solution:**

```swift
// ❌ Default: Performance SDK instruments all URLSession delegates
// This adds overhead and obscures crash-proximate frames.

// ✅ Disable automatic network instrumentation if not needed
// In Info.plist:
// <key>firebase_performance_collection_enabled</key>
// <false/>

// Or at runtime before FirebaseApp.configure():
Performance.sharedInstance().isInstrumentationEnabled = false
Performance.sharedInstance().isDataCollectionEnabled = false
```

## References

*   [Apple `URLSessionDataDelegate.urlSession(_:dataTask:didReceive:)`](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/urlsessiondatadelegate/urlsession(_:datatask:didreceive:)) — Documents that data callbacks deliver arbitrary chunks with no framing guarantees
*   [Apple `NSString.substring(with:)` Documentation](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/nsstring/substring(with:)) — Raises `NSRangeException` if the range is invalid
*   [firebase-ios-sdk Issue #14094](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk/issues/14094) — Root cause report: `RCNConfigRealtime` crash on fragmented JSON
*   [firebase-ios-sdk PR #14518](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk/pull/14518) — The merged fix ensuring `endRange.location` is searched after `beginRange.location`
*   [Firebase Remote Config CHANGELOG](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-ios-sdk/blob/master/FirebaseRemoteConfig/CHANGELOG.md) — Confirms fix shipped in 11.10.0

### Overview

During OTP login or account switch, the app clears the local Realm database by calling `AppDelegate.clearDb()`. This function was opening a Realm instance synchronously on the main thread, triggering Realm&#39;s one-time schema discovery (`+[RLMSchema sharedSchema]`) which iterates every Objective-C class loaded in the process. This blocked the UI for 1,750–1,896 ms on an iPhone 12, causing a user-visible hang during login. Across all versions, 58 hang logs (4% of total) were attributed to this code path.

**Source:** Xcode Organizer → Hangs → IndiaMART v13.6.4

